republican senator tom cotton of  arkansas joins us now from capitol hill  senator good morning good morning i want  to talk about daca and of course i know  you have a bill to address that are you  willing to grant those 800 000 children  legal status and what needs to be done  to achieve that  well first i want to say that president  trump took the right step yesterday  president obama's executive order was  illegal he said he didn't have the  authority before he issued it and he  said when he did issue it it was  temporary  but that said president trump is trying  to deal with the situation in the most  orderly way possible he can't defend  that order in court but he wants  congress to take action and i've talked  to him and i've said that i'm happy to  help try to forge some kind of  compromise if we're going to give legal  status though to these young people in  their 20s and 30s who are here through  no fault of their own we have to  recognize that's going to have negative  consequences it's going to open up a  whole new category of people who could  get legal status namely their parents  who violated the law by bringing them  here and it's going to encourage other  people to bring their children across  the border in the future which is a very  dangerous thing to do so my legislation  the raise act would stop unlimited  family chain migration addressing the  first problem and then enhanced  enforcement measures would address the  second problem that's a straightforward  and targeted package that doesn't try to  boil the ocean or solve every problem  but solves the problem right in front of  us  do you have any indication that  president trump supports your bill or  that the senate majority leader supports  your bill  well president trump and i uh along with  senator perdue my co-sponsor i had an  event last week  or last month at the white house where  we introduced the bill president trump  also  cited our legislation in his statement  yesterday  rescinding president obama's unlawful  order so he strongly supports it and  i've discussed with senator mcconnell my  efforts to try to help solve the  immediate problem in front of us but  also mitigate the harms of taking that  action congress has tried congress has  tried to pass a big sweeping immigration  bill three times in the last 11 years  it's failed every time it's time that we  take a different incremental common  sense approach which leads me to my  question where you said it's failed  every time it's heartbreaking looking at  the daca recipients who are really very  frightened they're terrified they're  marching in the streets the people that  love and care of them care about them  they really are in legal limbo so how  confident are you that this time  congress can get this done knowing  what's at stake well again if we address  this specific problem in front of us i'm  confident that we can part of the  problem has been that democrats for many  years have not wanted to take this  incremental measured step because they  wanted to try to tie up legal status for  the six to eight hundred thousand  twenty or thirty senators that leads us  right back to where we started the  democrats blame the republicans the  republicans blame the democrats so i  know you've had conversations with your  colleagues have there been real  heart-to-heart conversations about what  we're going to do to make sure this time  we get it done forget about the past we  now have to look to the future  well  actually there's plenty of blame to go  around to both parties on this and  failures three times over the last 11  years but no i've had conversations with  my democratic colleagues and you really  can't dispute just as a logical matter  that if we give legal status to these  people in their 20s and 30s that it is  going to encourage more illegal  immigration and it will open up legal  status for the very people who violated  the law so we should have an open and  common sense discussion about that and  try to solve those specific problems my  legislation the raise act does that and  say employment verification to make sure  that business owners aren't hiring  illegal immigrants will help address  that as well those are all details  though that we can negotiate if we sit  down and we focus on this specific  problem not everything under the sun  when it comes to immigration all right  senator tom cotton thank you very much  for your time appreciate it
 

